    ('transportation-priorities', 2, 'Rosener championed and publicly defended Sherwood''s $30 million pedestrian and bicycle bridge over Highway 99W (roughly $4 million in state lottery funds plus about $24-26 million in urban-renewal tax-increment financing), writing in May 2025 that it "represents more than just improved transportation; it''s a commitment to public safety, accessibility, and responsible growth," providing "a safe, ADA-compliant route for pedestrians and cyclists" and connecting schools and trail systems. His published mayoral priorities pair that multimodal investment with road work: championing "traffic calming and congestion solutions," ensuring student safety on roads and trails to schools, and securing state/federal funding for a grade-separated crossing at Cedar Creek. Committing major capital to walking/biking infrastructure alongside continued road and congestion investment matches the invest-in-both-roads-and-multimodal chair, not a transit-first posture with reduced parking.', ARRAY['https://rosener.com/therosenercorner/2025/1/9/zuhok11ucuf3ja521az38lu4sspokc-rwfft', 'https://rosener.com/priorities']::text[]),
